`fleet/referee-linker.mjs:378` resolves each candidate with `path.resolve(cloneDir || '.', rel)` and never checks that the result stays under `cloneDir`. A `files` entry of the form `../x.py` or an absolute path therefore becomes a candidate, and for a `.mjs` candidate the module is *executed* by the `import()` in the eval script — outside the task's clone. This is grounded only in my reading of the prose global constraint "A subprocess they spawn is `node` or `python3` on a file of the task's own clone" (no `Check:` was run against it), so it is minor; nothing in the twelve fixtures or the exam exercises an escaping path. How to fix, inside this task's own FILES: reject the entry before it becomes a candidate, alongside the existing `not in the clone` skip —

```
     const abs = path.resolve(cloneDir || '.', rel)
+    const root = path.resolve(cloneDir || '.')
+    if (abs !== root && !abs.startsWith(root + path.sep)) {
+      skipped.push(rel + ': outside the clone'); continue
+    }
     if (!isFile(abs)) { skipped.push(rel + ': not in the clone'); continue }
```

and add a leg asserting a `files` entry that escapes `cloneDir` answers `unlinked` with a stub `exec` that throws, proving no subprocess ran.; The timeout detail depends on `killed`/`signal`, two fields outside the seam shape the task's Context declares. `linkMjs` (`fleet/referee-linker.mjs:199`) and `linkPy` (`:242`) both test `res.killed || res.signal` to produce the `timeout after <ms>ms` detail, and only `defaultExec` (`:84-85`) sets those fields; the engine's own seam is specified as `exec(cmd, argv, {cwd, timeoutMs}) -> Promise<{code, stdout, stderr}>`. When the engine passes its seam and a candidate is killed at the timeout, the answer is still correctly `unlinked` (the `!out || res.code !== 0` branch at `:203`/`:246` catches it — the status M2 cares about never degrades to a finding), but if that seam reports an empty stderr the detail becomes `node exited <code>`, carrying neither the first line of stderr nor `timeout` — the two alternatives M2 names. The exam and the sim only exercise `defaultExec` for the timeout leg (`:1912` / exam `:719`), so this path is untested by construction. How to fix: either document `killed`/`signal` as optional extras of the seam in the `@param {function} [o.exec]` JSDoc at `:344-345` so the engine is obliged to supply them, or make the non-JSON branch name the bound it ran under (e.g. `'node produced no JSON within ' + timeoutMs + 'ms'`) when stderr is empty, so the detail is informative under either exec.; Footprint, outside FILES (informational): the captured patch carries `fleet/tests/exams/run_69/test_referee_linker.mjs`, a path not in this task's FILES. It is the driver's own exam handoff into the reserved exam directory (the peer-written exam graded by TEST COMMAND, EXAM EVIDENCE exit 0), not an implementer edit — every other touched path is in FILES, nothing is deleted, and no SIBLING FILES path (fleet/referee.mjs, the non-linker fixtures, fleet/tests/test_referee_replay.mjs) is touched. Recorded because the footprint rule asks the path be named; no edit inside this task's FILES answers it, so nothing to fix here.; Weak assertion in the sim, `fleet/tests/test_referee_linker.mjs` leg (a) `noExt`: it calls `link({bullet: '`foo(a)`', files: ['README.md', 'fleet/sandbox-boot.sh'], cloneDir: mjs1.dir})` and asserts `unlinked`, but neither file exists in `mjs1.dir` (which holds only `src/foo.mjs`). The answer would be `unlinked` from the not-in-the-clone branch even if the `LINKABLE` extension filter in `fleet/referee-linker.mjs:377` were deleted, so this particular case does not pin the M1 clause it names. The clause is still covered elsewhere — `linker-unlinked-2` has a real `fleet/sandbox-boot.sh` in its checkout and must answer `unlinked` — so no criterion is left unverified; the fix is to point the case at a clone where the non-linkable files actually exist.; concern: exam: the peer exam `fleet/tests/exams/run_69/test_referee_linker.mjs` carries two raw NUL bytes — the separator of `line.join(' ')` at its lines 285 and 377 is byte 0x00, not a space (first at byte offset 15894). The exam itself still passes (`joined.includes('file://')` and friends are unaffected), but the repo's own suite test `fleet/tests/test_no_binary_sources.mjs` fails on it: "source files must contain no raw NUL byte ... fleet/tests/exams/run_69/test_referee_linker.mjs (byte 15894)". That red is entirely the exam file's; my own files have zero NUL bytes (checked `fleet/referee-linker.mjs` and `fleet/tests/test_referee_linker.mjs`), and the failure predates my fix since the exam was staged before I began. I did not edit the exam — replacing those two bytes with spaces would fix `test_no_binary_sources.mjs` without changing any exam assertion, but that edit is the exam owner's to make.
